def GlobalDocumentation {
  code Intro =[{..
  -------------------------------------------------------------------
  NOTE: This file is automatically generated by running llvm-tblgen
  -gen-kit-func-attr-doc. Do not edit this file by hand!!
  -------------------------------------------------------------------

=================================
LLVM Function Attribute Reference
=================================
.. contents::
  :local:

.. |br| raw:: html

  <br/>

These are Kitsune-specific attributes that may be attached to LLVM functions.
These use the existing metadata mechanism available for `GlobalObject`'s (of
which `Function`'s are a subclass). This intentionally does not use the
function attribute mechanism for two reasons:

* The function attribute mechanism in LLVM only allows string-valued attributes.
  This would limit our ability to associate other objects with functions in the
  future if that is determined to be useful.

* Adding function attributes would require changes to be made to core LLVM each
  time we added a new attribute. This would result in a fairly expensive
  rebuild. Since Kitsune is primarily intended to be a research compiler, we
  anticipate adding new attributes fairly often. The compilation costs of such
  changes would be excessive.

Kitsune's function attributes must be one of the following kinds.

* **Flag Attributes**: These are attributes that do not take a value. Only
  their presence or absence is significant.

* **Valued Attributes**: These are attributes that take a value. Currently,
  only certain types of values are supported. These, together with the
  corresponding type that will appear in the LLVM-IR metadata are
  summarized in the table below.

  =============  ============
  Source type    LLVM-IR type
  =============  ============
  ``int32_t``    ``i32``
  ``int64_t``    ``i64``
  ``StringRef``  ``[n x i8]``
  =============  ============

* **Enum Attributes**: These are a special case of the valued attributes
  where the value type is an **enum** defined in Kitsune's source. The
  type of the value in LLVM-IR metadata will always be ``i32``.

In the example below, a flag attribute has been added to function `f`.
:ref:`kit.func.device <func-attr-device>`.

.. code :: kitll

  define !kit.func.device !0 void @f(i64 %n) {
    ret void
  }

  !0 = !{}

Every attribute has an entry in the ``FuncAttrKind`` enum.
}];
}

include "kitsune/Core/FuncAttrs.td"
